Now design the flake:

```nix
{
  description = "HackTheBurgh 2026 development environment";

  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      systems = [ "x86_64-linux" "aarch64-linux" "x86_64-darwin" "aarch64-darwin" ];
      forEachSystem = f: nixpkgs.lib.genAttrs systems (system: f nixpkgs.legacyPackages.${system});
    in {
      devShells = forEachSystem (pkgs: {
        default = pkgs.mkShell {
          packages = with pkgs; [ nodejs_24 pnpm uv ruff python314 jq curl git gnumake ];
          env = { PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH = ...; };
          shellHook = ''...'';
        };
      });
    };
}
```

Considerations:
- python314 exists in nixpkgs unstable? Probably `python314` now (2026). If not, `python3` is 3.13/3.14. Let me check `nix eval nixpkgs#python314.version`.
- Include uv; uv will use .python-version 3.14 and can use the nix-provided python if in PATH. Better: put python314 in PATH so uv finds a compatible interpreter without downloading.
- ruff: available both via uv dev group and nixpkgs; include `ruff` so `uv run ruff` works offline-ish. Actually `uv run ruff` uses the project's dev group; keep nix ruff optional but harmless. Could omit to avoid duplicate.
- Node: package.json has packageManager pnpm@10.32.1. corepack in node 24 enables pnpm download from npm (network). Alternatively pin pnpm from nixpkgs (10.32.1? nixpkgs pnpm version may differ). Using nixpkgs pnpm avoids corepack download. But packageManager field + corepack mismatch: corepack would complain? If PNPM is on PATH, pnpm works directly; corepack shims may still intercept if enabled. Simplest: include both nodejs_24 and pnpm from nixpkgs; set COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT=0, and mention.

Hmm, packageManager field "pnpm@10.32.1" — corepack only intervenes if enabled. Since pnpm binary from nixpkgs exists, fine.

- Playwright: needs browsers. nixpkgs has `playwright-driver.browsers` (chromium etc.) and `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`. Version must match the playwright package version used by the web app. That's fragile. Better: don't include browsers; set `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD`? Actually simpler: skip, and note in the shellHook comment. But web/AGENTS requires Playwright browser checks for frontend work. Including nixpkgs playwright browsers with a mismatched version breaks. Alternative: let pnpm install playwright and run `pnpm exec playwright install chromium` into the default cache, which needs system libs. Nix can provide the libs via `playwright-driver.browsers`... 

Practical compromise: include `chromium` from nixpkgs? Playwright can't easily use system chromium unless channel set. Too fragile for a hackathon. I'll leave browsers out but add the libs needed? No.

Keep the flake tight: git, nodejs_24, pnpm, uv, python314, jq, curl, httpie? no. Maybe `duckdb`? no. `psql`? no.

Also: `google-chrome`? no.

Include `gnumake`, `gcc` (in case a node native module needs building), `pkg-config`.

Also CI-ish: `nix flake check`? Add `formatter` = nixfmt? Nice touch: `formatter = pkgs.nixfmt-rfc-style;`. Keep.

shellHook: print banner, set COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT=0, `export UV_PYTHON_PREFERENCE=only-system`? If python314 in PATH, uv uses it. Setting `UV_PYTHON=python3.14` might conflict. Better: `export UV_PYTHON_DOWNLOADS=never` to force using nix python if present, avoiding surprise downloads. Hmm, if nix python314 is present it's fine. I'll set UV_PYTHON_PREFERENCE=only-system so uv won't fetch — deterministic, and it errors loudly if missing.

Also add `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`? Playwright/others no.

Now: flake.lock. Building the flake requires network to fetch nixpkgs (github). Lix can do it. Should I generate flake.lock? A lockfile is needed for reproducibility; `nix flake lock` will fetch. Might be slow but let's try; also `nix develop --command true` to verify the shell evaluates.

Careful: evaluation might take a while (downloading nixpkgs tarball). Let's try with a generous timeout, background if needed.

Check python314 availability first, then write flake, then lock + test.

Also should I add `.envrc` for direnv with `use flake`? Optional; harmless. The user asked just for flake. I'll skip direnv, maybe mention.

Also, since AGENTS says "any version pin is recorded with its reason" — that's for package versions in package manifests. I'll add a README-ish comment in flake.nix explaining nodejs_24 (matches the local node 24.21) and python314 (matches .python-version).

